WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.067 line:92%
-You mentioned Peter Thiel.

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's this big
Silicon Valley guy.

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:04.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
He had personal gripes
with Gawker.

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:06.873
-That's right.
-This was him using Hulk Hogan,

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
who he did not have
any relationship with,

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:10.944 align:start size:94% position:6%
basically,
to take vengeance on Gawker.

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:13.613 align:start size:94% position:6%
Interestingly, he's also a big
part of the Trump campaign.

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:15.482 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's a supporter
of Donald Trump.

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:18.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now we have a president,
who, I feel,

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
has the same idea
about the press

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Peter Thiel had
about Gawker.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:23.156 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Is there a fear now that

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:25.725
Donald Trump will be inspired
by what happened with Gawker

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.228 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that he will say,
"Oh, you know, basically,

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:30.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you have the legal team
with more money,

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can basically crush
these smaller news sources"?

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:34.467 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Absolutely. I think
there is a fear about that --

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
this basic
sort of chilling effect

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:39.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it might have
on news organizations.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:40.240 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Yeah. And part of it

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that Peter Thiel did this
in secret, right?

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
So he went after these people
in secret.

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:45.612 line:92%
And -- And at the same time,

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:48.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
you could never quite separate
the case

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:49.149 line:92%
from this crazy election cycle.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.884
I mean, this is at the same time
Trump is saying --

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.386
you know, pointing at the press
at his rallies and speeches

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
and saying, "We're gonna,
you know, open up libel laws

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:58.091 align:start size:94% position:6%
and sue you like you've
never been seen sued before."

00:00:58.091 --> 00:00:59.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean,
he's calling the press scum

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:03.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
and horrible, horrible people
and all of that.

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.064 line:92%
So -- And then, you know,

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:06.399
Peter Thiel became
a Trump supporter and all that.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:07.901 line:92%
So, yeah, you know, the --

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:10.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
"USA Today"
did a great story where --

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
that looked
at the 3,500 lawsuits

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.807 line:92%
that Trump is involved with,

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:16.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I think
it's a very real concern.

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:20.680
-I find it very depressing
that a media organization,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.581 size:84% position:42% line:92%
any media organization,

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:24.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
can be put out of business
by a lawsuit.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yet, there is --
in this era of Trump,

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
there are some, like,
positive signs of journalism.

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:29.489 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:31.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"New York Times,"
"Washington Post" --

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:32.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
both have subscription bases
going up.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.462
Do you feel like there is this
unique opportunity now for --

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:37.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
even at a time
where they're under attack --

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:38.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
for journalism
to sort of rise up

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:42.669
and maybe use this Trump moment
and this time we're living in

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:44.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be more robust?
-Yeah. Absolutely.

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:47.340
I do actually think that's one
of the sort of silver linings

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
of this Trump era,
this early time.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I think, you know,
people have --

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's legitimate criticism
of media

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:56.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's gotten too corporatized
over time,

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:58.251 line:92%
maybe got too cozy with power,

00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:00.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it trades, you know,
softball stories

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
for access to power
and celebrity.

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:04.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know, I think that
you see the "Washington Post,"

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:05.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you see
"The New York Times,"

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:07.694
and you're starting to see
really great journalism.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:08.428 size:84% position:42% line:92%
So there's like this --

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:10.530
they're kind of remembering
what the press is there for.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.499 size:84% position:42% line:92%
I mean, look, you know,

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:14.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
journalism is,
almost by definition,

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:17.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
something that makes
powerful people uncomfortable,

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.138 line:92%
and that's the point, right?

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:19.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
Speaking truth to power
is the point,

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:22.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we need this
in a participatory democracy.

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:24.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think
if we're sort of remembering

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
what that's all about,
why it's important,

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:27.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
that can actually be
a good thing.

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:28.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well,
I hope that comes to pass,

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
and congratulations
with the documentary.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:31.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a great story,
very well told.

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you very much
for being here.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.554
-Thanks.
-Brian Knappenberger, everybody.

